# env files — NONE are committed.
#
# There are exactly two (2026-08-18): `.env` for local development and `.env.production` for
# release builds, which is layered on top of `.env` when NODE_ENV=production. Cloud builds don't
# read either — their values live in eas.json > build.<profile>.env and the EAS env store.
#
# These were once listed here while also being *tracked*, which does nothing: .gitignore is only
# consulted for untracked paths. They were untracked with `git rm --cached` (2026-08-13) so these
# rules finally bite.
.env
.env.*

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo

# (Removed a stray bare `example` rule here — it matched any file or directory named exactly
# `example` at any depth, which was almost certainly a typo for `.env.example` and would have
# silently swallowed a real `example/` folder. It matched nothing, so dropping it is a no-op.)

# generated native folders
/ios
/android

# Firebase
#
# `google-services.json` is deliberately NOT ignored. It is Firebase *client* config, not a
# credential: it is compiled into the APK, so anyone holding the app already has it. Its API key
# is restricted to this Android package + signing certificate and grants no server access.
# Committing it is what lets a fresh clone (and an EAS cloud build) produce a push-capable
# build with no extra setup. `app.config.js` also accepts a GOOGLE_SERVICES_JSON path if you
# would rather supply it as an EAS file secret instead.
#
# The Firebase **service-account key** is the opposite — it is a private key that can send push
# to every user and read the whole project. It belongs to the BACKEND, never to this repo. These
# patterns exist so that downloading one to the wrong folder can't quietly commit it:
*service-account*.json
*serviceaccount*.json
*-firebase-adminsdk-*.json
